<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Package sources for this repo.

  This file exists because package-mode restore (-p:JauntyQUsePackages=true, the
  mode CI builds in) had no source carrying JauntyQ.Generator / JauntyQ.Runtime.
  It worked on a developer machine anyway, because ~/.nuget/packages was already
  populated by a local `dotnet pack`, so the cache was standing in for the feed
  and the gap only ever surfaced on a clean runner, as:

    error NU1101: Unable to find package JauntyQ.Generator. No packages exist
    with this id in source(s): ..., Microsoft Visual Studio Offline Packages,
    nuget.org

  The packages are published to GitHub Packages by jauntyq's own
  .github/workflows/publish.yml on v* tags.

  Note for anyone editing this file: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a
  comment, so write dashes as commas or semicolons here.
-->
<configuration>

  <packageSources>
    <!-- Clear the machine-level sources so a restore resolves identically here
         and on a runner. Without it the set depends on what the local SDK and
         Visual Studio installs happen to have registered. -->
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    <add key="jauntyq-github" value="https://nuget.pkg.github.com/extrode/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!-- No packageSourceCredentials section here, deliberately. GitHub Packages
       needs auth even to read, but a token belongs in the CI secret store, not
       in a committed file: ci.yml runs `dotnet nuget add source` against the
       runner's OWN user config with the job's GITHUB_TOKEN just before restore,
       so the token never touches this file. It was `update source` with no
       ;;configfile until 2026-09-05, which wrote the token here.

       To build in package mode LOCALLY, put the credential in your own user
       config, never here. Verified 2026-08-17; dashes doubled as ";;" below
       because this is an XML comment, so drop the semicolons when typing:

         gh auth refresh -h github.com -s read:packages
         dotnet nuget add source https://nuget.pkg.github.com/extrode/index.json
           ;;name jauntyq-github ;;username extrode
           ;;password "$(gh auth token)"
           ;;configfile "$env:APPDATA\NuGet\NuGet.Config"

       A gh OAuth token (gho_) is accepted by the NuGet registry, so no classic
       PAT is needed. On Windows the password is stored DPAPI encrypted, so the
       file holds no readable token and does not travel to another machine.

       Two things to know when it breaks. This copies the gh token rather than
       reading it live, so if gh ever reissues one the copy goes stale and
       restore fails with 401, saying nothing about gh; re-run the add against
       the same config file to overwrite. And the source goes in the user config
       only for its credentials: the <clear /> above drops it from the resolved
       source list, while packageSourceCredentials is a separate section that
       <clear /> does not touch, so the entry still authenticates the source
       defined here. -->

  <!-- Source mapping does two jobs. It stops a JauntyQ.* id from ever being
       resolved off nuget.org, where a same-named package would be someone
       else's code; and because a source is only queried for ids that map to it,
       it keeps a restore from touching the authenticated source for anything
       but JauntyQ.*. Since 2026-09-05 the default IS package mode, so an
       ordinary `dotnet restore` does query it: either the pinned versions are
       already in ~/.nuget/packages, or the source needs a credential in your
       own user config.

       Mapping is all or nothing: once this section exists, an id matching no
       pattern fails to resolve. Hence the "*" catch-all. -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="jauntyq-github">
      <package pattern="JauntyQ.*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
